Datetime Objects
Key Points
The datetime module contains several classes, including time, data, datetime, and timedelta.

Datetime methods allow you to modify how you represent date and time objects.

The datetime module is used to create a time object. It contains several classes, including:

Time 

Used to represent a time of the day
All times are represented in 24-hour format
Seconds and minutes have to be between zero and 59. Microseconds have to be between zero and less than a million. 
Date 

Used to represent a day of the year
Datetime 

Used to represent a calendar event
Timedelta 

Measures time elapsed
Produced whenever you subtract one datetime object from another
Has attributes for days, seconds, and microseconds
Timedelta objects can also be made from the constructor function
Can be used to specify hours and weeks
Can’t specify months because they don’t all have the same amount of days, or years, because of the same reason
Very useful in checking timespans 
Example:

>>> limit = datetime.timedelta(weeks=4)

>>> d = datetime.datetime(2019,9,25,13,15,45)

>>> e = datetime.datetime(2019,10,12,11,55)

>>> e-d > limit

False

Strftime 

Datetime method used to represent strings
Lets you customize the format you use to represent date and time objects
Example:

>>> d = datetime.datetime(2019,9,25,13,24,15)

>>> d.strftime('%Y-%m-%d')

'2019-09-25'

Isoformat 

Method for representing time in ISO 8601, the format used in most official datasets
Example:

>>> d = datetime.datetime(2019,9,25,13,15,45)

>>> d.isoformat()

'2019-09-25T13:15:45'

dateutil.parser

Not a part of the datetime module, but rather a separate module used to recover a datetime object from a string
Its parse function can construct the object using a date or time in any format
Example:

>>> from dateutil.parser import parse

>>> parse('May 9, 2019')

datetime.datetime(2019,5,9,0,0)